in order to build a fair lock your basic hw primitive has to be fair. At this point I ll turn heel and redefine fairness as a guarantee
that the lock will be acquired in a bounded #steps. I *believe* this is the community definition for wait-free algorithms but not sure.
But since the term is so cool lets henceforth use the term "wait-free" to denote guaranteed eventual completion.

Now my FifoSleepQueue and GroupFairSpinLock are both fair spin locks. The latter is clever in the sense it uses bounded fairness
while the former is more aggressive in the sense that it allows locks to be only acquired in strict FIFO order.


WORRY:
The basic building block for both these algorithms is a Java AtomicInteger.getAndIncrement() Lets assume the Java implementation 
does not use synchronized and instead uses hw lock:addAndIncrement your fairness is based on the argument that lock:addAndIncrement
instruction in hw is wait-free.

I am convinced that on SPARC (and possibly other RISCs like POWER) this *CANNOT* be wait-free because its implemented in microcode
using CAS / try and increment.

Is it wait-free on x86? Don't know.


SOLUTION:
Depending upon AtomicInteger.getAndIncrement() is turning out to be a worry for two reasons:
-> you are depending upon the processor to implement a highly scalable ADD_AND_INCREMENT implementation. This will be the hotlock
in the processor so consider a true 16 way system where this instruction is contended often I wonder how efficient this implementation
will be using Intels MESI protocol.

-> The second worry being the worse of the two. What if lock:addAndIncrement is *NOT* wait-free?

I think we can solve this by using either some form of a clock time value (since it progressively increments) or some sort of a 
cycle count feature like rdtsc. So take the value mod and bound it and use the GroupFairSpinLock principle.